# Jira Access — API Token Only (Never MCP)

## Hard Rule

When the user (or any task) requires reading or writing to Jira (issues, comments, transitions, attachments, JQL search, project metadata, etc.), the agent **must**:

1. Use the **Atlassian REST API directly** via `curl` with **email + API token Basic Auth**.
2. Read credentials from **environment variables in `~/.zshrc`**.
3. **Never** call the Atlassian MCP server for Jira operations (no `mcp_auth`, no MCP tool calls for Jira). The Atlassian MCP is unreliable in this environment and frequently fails OAuth.

This applies to **every** Jira interaction, including:

- Reading issue details, comments, history, attachments
- Creating or updating issues
- Adding comments
- Transitioning status (e.g., To Do → In Progress)
- Linking issues, adding labels/components
- Searching with JQL
- Fetching changelogs or worklogs
- Project / board / sprint queries

## Standard Configuration

### Required environment variables (in `~/.zshrc`)

```bash
export JIRA_BASE_URL="https://workdaybt.atlassian.net"
export JIRA_EMAIL="[REDACTED_EMAIL]"
export JIRA_API_TOKEN=[REDACTED]
```

Generate the API token at: <https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens>

If `JIRA_API_TOKEN` is not set, **stop and ask the user to set it** — do not fall back to the MCP, do not prompt for OAuth.

### Verify the credentials are loaded before any call

```bash
[[ -n "$JIRA_BASE_URL" && -n "$JIRA_EMAIL" && -n "$JIRA_API_TOKEN" ]] \
  && echo "Jira creds loaded" \
  || { echo "Jira creds missing"; exit 1; }
```

## Standard `curl` Patterns

### Get an issue (with comments)

```bash
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  -H "Accept: application/json" \
  "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620?fields=summary,status,assignee,reporter,comment" \
  | jq '{key,summary:.fields.summary,status:.fields.status.name,assignee:.fields.assignee.displayName,reporter:.fields.reporter.displayName,comments:[.fields.comment.comments[] | {author:.author.displayName, created:.created, updated:.updated, body:.body}]}'
```

### Get only the latest comment

```bash
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  -H "Accept: application/json" \
  "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620/comment?orderBy=-created&maxResults=1" \
  | jq '.comments[0] | {author:.author.displayName, created, updated, body}'
```

### Filter comments by author (e.g., latest from "Tasha")

```bash
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  -H "Accept: application/json" \
  "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620/comment?orderBy=-created&maxResults=100" \
  | jq '[.comments[] | select(.author.displayName | test("Tasha";"i"))] | .[0] | {author:.author.displayName, created, body}'
```

### Add a comment

```bash
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  -H "Accept: application/json" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -X POST "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620/comment" \
  --data '{
    "body": {
      "type": "doc", "version": 1,
      "content": [{
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Override deployed to QA, validated end-to-end." }]
      }]
    }
  }'
```

### Transition an issue

```bash
# 1) Discover available transitions
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620/transitions" | jq '.transitions[] | {id,name}'

# 2) Apply by transition id
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -X POST "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620/transitions" \
  --data '{"transition":{"id":"<TRANSITION_ID>"}}'
```

### JQL search

```bash
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  -G "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/search" \
  --data-urlencode 'jql=project = EDAEM AND status != Done AND assignee = currentUser() ORDER BY updated DESC' \
  --data-urlencode 'fields=summary,status,priority,updated' \
  | jq '.issues[] | {key,summary:.fields.summary,status:.fields.status.name,updated:.fields.updated}'
```

### Get issue worklogs / changelog

```bash
# Worklogs
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620/worklog" | jq '.worklogs[]'

# Changelog (status history etc.)
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620?expand=changelog&fields=summary" \
  | jq '.changelog.histories[] | {author:.author.displayName, created, items}'
```

## ADF Body Helper

Jira Cloud comments require the Atlassian Document Format. Quick converter for plain text → ADF JSON:

```bash
text_to_adf() {
  local text="$1"
  jq -n --arg t "$text" '{
    type:"doc", version:1,
    content:[{type:"paragraph", content:[{type:"text", text:$t}]}]
  }'
}

# Usage
curl -sS -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -X POST "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/issue/EDAEM-3620/comment" \
  --data "$(jq -n --argjson body "$(text_to_adf 'Override deployed; finance to validate.')" '{body:$body}')"
```

## Forbidden Patterns

Do **not** do any of the following for Jira work:

- Call `mcp_auth` for the `atlassian` MCP server.
- Invoke any tool under `project-*-atlassian` MCP server.
- Tell the user "let me authenticate the Atlassian MCP first".
- Open browser-based OAuth flows for Jira via Cursor.
- Use the Atlassian Confluence MCP for Confluence operations either — Confluence should also use API token via `curl` (extend this rule pattern).

If a tool / subagent attempts an MCP call for Jira, **abort and switch to the curl + API token pattern**.

## Why This Rule Exists

- The Atlassian MCP requires an interactive OAuth flow that is unreliable in headless / agent contexts.
- API token + Basic Auth works deterministically from any shell, including subagents and CI.
- Avoids stalling on auth prompts the user has to manually approve every session.
- Token-based access is auditable and revocable from the Atlassian admin console.

## Verification Checklist (run once per session, before first Jira call)

1. `echo "$JIRA_BASE_URL"` returns the URL.
2. `echo "$JIRA_EMAIL"` returns the email.
3. `[[ -n "$JIRA_API_TOKEN" ]] && echo "token present" || echo "token MISSING"`.
4. Smoke test:
   ```bash
   curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" \
     -u "$JIRA_EMAIL:$JIRA_API_TOKEN" \
     "$JIRA_BASE_URL/rest/api/3/myself"
   # expect 200
   ```

If smoke test returns 401/403, ask the user to refresh the API token.
